    "section": "factuality_and_math_checks",
    "content": "Embedded premise verification gate — run when the user prompt contains a factual assumption baked into the question (signaled by 'if X, will Y?', 'since X is true...', 'given that X...', or questions that presuppose a specific framing). Procedure: (1) Extract each embedded premise and write: 'Premise: [X]. Assessment: [correct / partially flawed / incorrect]. Reason: [brief justification].' (2) If a premise is flawed: a candidate that correctly identifies and qualifies or rejects the flawed premise earns CREDIT for accuracy — do not penalize it for 'not answering the question as asked.' (3) A candidate that uncritically affirms a flawed premise and builds elaborate content on top of it is LESS correct, regardless of how detailed, well-organized, or authoritative it sounds. Detailed affirmation of a wrong premise is a worse answer than concise correction of it. (4) This gate feeds into the factual pre-determination gate — a flawed premise changes what constitutes the 'correct answer.'"
  },
